{"text": "Three Suspects.\n\nElif read the half card again and again. It said \"four eggs,\" \"one glass of sugar\" and \"two lemons.\" The other half was missing.\n\nSomebody broke the card in two. Did they keep the other half?\n\nShe opened her notebook. \"Clue two: half a card in Limon's basket.\"\n\nThen she wrote three names under the clues. Bora. Theo. Mr Selim.\n\nShe liked all three people. That was the worst part.\n\nBora was Elif's cousin. He was seventeen, and he loved his phone. He took photos of every plate in the restaurant. Then he put them online.\n\n\"Did you take the recipe card?\" Elif asked him.\n\n\"Why? I don't need a card,\" said Bora. \"I only need photos.\"\n\nHe showed her a photo of the lemon cake. It had fifty thousand likes.\n\nBut Elif noticed something. Bora's hands were white with flour.\n\n\"I helped with the bread this morning,\" he said quickly.\n\nToo quickly? Elif wrote it down.\n\nTheo was the new waiter. He came from England, and he was learning Turkish. He was polite and quiet. He always carried a small blue notebook. All day, he wrote new words in it.\n\n\"Theo, were you in the kitchen yesterday?\" Elif asked.\n\n\"Yes. Every morning,\" said Theo. His Turkish was slow but careful. \"I come very early.\"\n\n\"Why so early? The restaurant opens at ten.\"\n\nTheo smiled, but he didn't answer. He closed his notebook a little faster.\n\nMr Selim had the cake shop across the street. He was the kindest man in Ayvalık. He gave sweets to every child.\n\n\"Your aunt's recipe? I'd love to see it!\" he laughed. \"But I make my own cakes. And mine are sweeter.\"\n\nElif compared her suspects in her notebook. Bora was the busiest. Theo was the quietest. Mr Selim was the kindest. Nobody looked like a thief.\n\nLater that evening, Theo left his notebook on a table. It was open. Elif didn't want to read it. But she saw one page.\n\nThere was a drawing of a lemon. Next to it, there was a time: six in the morning.", "textSha256": "d35174eb00042